# Core (read first)
Layout: `playbook-*.mdc` = generic dev playbook (copyable to other projects). `project-*.mdc` = TravelAgency specifics (override the playbook where stated). Controller: `.cursor/agents/orchestrator.md`. Cloud personas: `.cursor/briefs/` (pasted into a launch; not separate bots).

- Language: talk to the owner in Russian; agent-to-agent in English. Repo artifacts (commits, PRs, issues, comments, wiki, code comments): Russian.
- Inside `.cursor/` (rules, `.cursor/agents/`, `.cursor/briefs/`, templates): write what the process needs. Owner, orchestrator, Cursor Automations, cloud agents, and brief names are fine. `AGENTS.md` stays a short pointer to these files and does not repeat the rules.
- Outside `.cursor/` (wiki, issues, PRs, commits, comments, code comments, and any other public text): impersonal Russian by default. No personal names, no role labels as people, no agent or role as the actor, no «я/мы сделали», no `Co-authored-by` for bots. Write «принято решение», «добавлено», «проверено». When clarity needs it, name Cursor Automations. Do not name a role, a person, or another agent as the actor. No names of retired chat-bots.
- Secrets: `playbook-security.mdc`. Never print, commit or paste one.
- Default at merge: if the owner already put the task in work or explicitly authorized that work, and the PR matches that scope, merge without a separate yes and notify the owner after merge.
- Ask the owner before merge only when the PR hits one of these and that exact case is not already authorized: irreversible or large-scale work (global delete, secrets, server changes, repo settings or access, history rewrite or force-push, spending money); Dependabot for docker or github-actions, including grouped updates that may contain majors; work outside the authorized scope; an unclear breaking change; a peer conflict; red CI.
- npm and NuGet Dependabot bumps, including majors: merge when CI is green and nothing needs clarification, then notify (`playbook-git-pr.mdc`). A peer conflict, red CI, or an unclear breaking change still blocks that merge.
- Team approval requests go through the orchestrator.
- Results over process: read only what the task needs, minimal diff, stop at acceptance criteria, no gold-plating. Report: outcome first with links, then only decisions/risks.

